e-block leakage outside V_p + V_{2p}^prim. (b) **S148's Gallagher-variance framing is REFUTED for the main term**: the main term is a **Ramanujan-sum / principal-character-of-Dirichlet quantity**, NOT a 2nd-moment of primes. The Gallagher variance enters only in the sub-leading remainder. These are different objects governed by different machinery (Ramanujan-sum c_q(k) = mu(q) — deterministic; Gallagher variance — Bombieri-Vinogradov 2nd-moment). (c) **SVD spike-block leakage diagnosed**: empirical `sum sigma²_top-spikes` exceeds `E(p, N)` by 4-38% at d=20 (p=3 / 5 / 7), shrinking with N (e.g., p=3: 18% at d=14 → 4% at d=20); asymptotically the SVD spike block converges to the V_p ⊕ V_{2p}^prim subspace energy. (d) **Sharper structural identification**: chi_P's MPS-unfolding spike content at conductor 2p is **EXACTLY** the principal-Dirichlet-character contribution to mod-p / mod-2p prime distribution, with all character L-function content sitting in the sub-leading remainder. This is sharper than S82's "approximate eigenvector ~ |L(1, chi)|²" claim (refuted in S148) AND sharper than S148's "Gallagher variance" claim. **Closure mode C** (circular): the theorem proves what the spike-block energy IS, but computing E(p, N) requires pi(N) and the remainder requires Gallagher 2nd-moment data. The C-circular collapse stands; what's new is the *exactness* of the analytic identification of the main term. Cites E2.1, E1.5, S74, S82, S148. Cross-domain refs: Hardy-Wright (2008) *An Intro to the Theory of Numbers* Ch. 16 (Ramanujan sums); Iwaniec-Kowalski (2004) *Analytic Number Theory* Ch. 3 (Dirichlet character orthogonality, Gauss sums); Davenport (2000) *MNT* Ch. 6, 8 (PNT-in-AP); Gallagher (1970) *Mathematika* 23 (used only for remainder bound); Montgomery-Vaughan (2007) *MNT I* Ch. 9 (Gauss sums), Ch. 17 (Var of primes in AP). See experiments/constructions/spike_gallagher_proof/. | 166 |
